+Executable incremental model and limits
+
+Let G=(V,E) be the immutable acyclic typed expression graph in source order. Each
+non-input node v holds f_v(values(pred(v))), evaluated with the specified ordered fp64
+operations. Values are finite tensors; each f_v is deterministic for a fixed toolchain
+and floating-point environment. Literal input names are distinct, including equal literals.
+
+An event changes one admitted input, schedules its users and processes the smallest
+node index first. If recomputation has identical shape and element bit patterns, its
+users need not be scheduled. Otherwise, its direct users are scheduled at most once.
+By topological induction, after the queue drains each affected node equals full evaluation
+on the new inputs. Descendants of a bit-identical result remain valid. This reasoning
+requires purity, acyclicity, complete dependency edges and consistent numeric semantics;
+it does not apply to arbitrary external effects, clocks, random calls or in-place aliases.
+
+Before replacement the old Value is moved into a bounded transaction record. A runtime
+fault or failed assertion restores those records in reverse order. A committed event
+retains that record as one-level undo. This uses memory proportional to changed values:
+there is NO zero-byte undo claim. The input stream is ordered; concurrent writes require
+a separately specified protocol. This implementation is NOT a distributed consensus or
+replication implementation and does not inherit ERA's unrelated fixed-point proofs.
+
+Exact structural expression reuse uses (opcode,left-id,right-id), never a hash-only hit
+or approximate numeric equality. Literal inputs are not interned. Commutative operand
+sorting, reassociation and replacement of multiplication+addition by FMA are prohibited.
+No claim of invention follows: change propagation, memoization, transactional rollback
+and incremental computation have substantial prior art. Evidence files report measured
+cases, and are not formal proofs of implementation correctness for every possible input.
